<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Morazán</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Morazán was a Honduran general and Liberal leader who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> in 1829 and was president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> from 1830 to 1839. He tried to hold the federation together against Conservative revolts, but it broke up into separate states. Seeking to restore it, he seized Costa Rica in 1842 and was overthrown and shot.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan.jpg" alt="Francisco Morazán" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aquiles Bigot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Moraz%C3%A1n_Quesada.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Honduran liberal <b>Francisco Morazán</b> emerged as the outstanding commander, winning the Battle of La Trinidad in November 1827 and the Battle of Gualcho in 1828, and on 13 April 1829 his allied army took Guatemala City.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Rafael Carrera’s conservatives defeated <b>Francisco Morazán</b> at Guatemala City in March 1840, the federation collapsed, and on 18 February 1841 a constituent assembly adopted a constitution declaring El Salvador a sovereign and independent state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Carrera’s forces took Guatemala City in 1838 and defeated the federal president <b>Francisco Morazán</b> there in March 1840, ending the federation in practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It was also the homeland of <b>Francisco Morazán</b>, the liberal general who led the federation as its president from 1830 to 1839.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ximénez's manuscripts stayed with the Dominican Order after his death until General <b>Francisco Morazán</b> expelled the clergy from Guatemala in 1829–30, when the Order's documents passed largely to the Universidad de San Carlos.</blockquote><a href="/work/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Carrillo was overthrown in 1842 by <b>Francisco Morazán</b>, who was himself executed months later.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-costa-rica">State of Costa Rica</a></li></ul></section>
